// Original GRE Verbal question bank: Text Completion (VERBAL_TC) and Sentence Equivalence (VERBAL_SE).
// Plain browser script (no modules). Blanks are written as {1}, {2}, {3}.
window.VERBAL_TC = [
  // ---------- One-blank items ----------
  { id:"tc001", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:1,
    text:"Although the senator was known for long-winded speeches, her remarks at the ceremony were surprisingly {1}, lasting barely two minutes.",
    options:[["prolix","laconic","florid","garrulous","digressive"]],
    answer:[1],
    explanation:"\"Although\" sets up a contrast with \"long-winded,\" and \"barely two minutes\" confirms brevity, so the blank needs a word meaning using few words: laconic. Prolix, florid, garrulous, and digressive all describe the long-windedness the sentence says was absent." },
  { id:"tc002", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:1,
    text:"The critic, who usually reserved his praise for only the most accomplished works, greeted the young novelist's debut with unqualified {1}.",
    options:[["skepticism","indifference","approbation","derision","ambivalence"]],
    answer:[2],
    explanation:"The clause about reserving \"praise\" for accomplished works signals that this debut earned praise; \"unqualified\" means wholehearted. Approbation (approval) fits. Ambivalence cannot be \"unqualified,\" and skepticism, indifference, and derision are not praise." },
  { id:"tc003", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:1,
    text:"Because the fossil evidence was so {1}, consisting of a single tooth and a fragment of jaw, paleontologists were cautious about naming a new species.",
    options:[["abundant","meager","conclusive","pristine","contested"]],
    answer:[1],
    explanation:"\"A single tooth and a fragment of jaw\" defines the blank: the evidence was scanty, or meager, which explains the caution. Abundant and conclusive contradict the description; contested is not supported by anything in the sentence; pristine describes condition, not quantity." },
  { id:"tc004", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:1,
    text:"The archivist's {1} approach to cataloguing (she checked every date against three independent sources) made her records the most reliable in the department.",
    options:[["desultory","cursory","haphazard","sedulous","perfunctory"]],
    answer:[3],
    explanation:"Checking every date against three sources is painstaking, and the result is reliability, so the blank means diligent: sedulous. Desultory, cursory, haphazard, and perfunctory all describe careless or superficial work, the opposite of what the parenthesis describes." },
  { id:"tc005", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:1,
    text:"Far from being {1}, the new policy was expected to remain in force for decades.",
    options:[["ephemeral","durable","entrenched","prudent","popular"]],
    answer:[0],
    explanation:"\"Far from\" signals that the blank is the opposite of \"remain in force for decades,\" so it must mean short-lived: ephemeral. Durable and entrenched mean the same as lasting and would make the sentence contradict itself; prudent and popular are unrelated to duration." },
  { id:"tc006", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:1,
    text:"Despite repeated appeals from his advisers, the general remained {1}, refusing to alter his plan even slightly.",
    options:[["tractable","vacillating","contrite","pliant","intransigent"]],
    answer:[4],
    explanation:"\"Refusing to alter his plan even slightly\" defines the blank: intransigent (unwilling to compromise). Tractable and pliant mean easily persuaded, the opposite; vacillating means wavering; contrite means remorseful." },
  { id:"tc007", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:1,
    text:"The documentary's narrator speaks in a flat, {1} voice that drains even the most dramatic events of their excitement.",
    options:[["impassioned","strident","mercurial","monotonous","sonorous"]],
    answer:[3],
    explanation:"\"Flat\" and \"drains... of their excitement\" call for a word continuing that idea: monotonous. Impassioned and strident suggest intensity; mercurial suggests rapid change; sonorous means rich and resonant, which would add drama rather than drain it." },
  { id:"tc008", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:1,
    text:"Rather than {1} the crisis, the new regulations only made it worse, as lenders withdrew even further from the market.",
    options:[["exacerbating","prolonging","complicating","publicizing","mitigating"]],
    answer:[4],
    explanation:"\"Rather than\" contrasts the blank with \"made it worse,\" so the blank means eased: mitigating. Exacerbating, prolonging, and complicating all describe making things worse, which leaves no contrast; publicizing is irrelevant." },
  { id:"tc009", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:1,
    text:"The once-thriving port town has {1} steadily since the railway bypassed it, its population now a fraction of what it was a century ago.",
    options:[["burgeoned","flourished","stabilized","waned","rallied"]],
    answer:[3],
    explanation:"\"Once-thriving\" and a population that is \"a fraction\" of what it was point to decline: waned. Burgeoned, flourished, and rallied indicate growth or recovery; stabilized contradicts \"steadily\" shrinking." },
  { id:"tc010", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:2,
    text:"Many readers found the novelist's later prose {1}; where her early stories had been spare and exact, her final books sprawl across hundreds of pages of digression.",
    options:[["austere","prolix","terse","cogent","pithy"]],
    answer:[1],
    explanation:"The semicolon introduces an explanation: the later books \"sprawl\" with \"digression,\" in contrast to the early \"spare and exact\" stories. Prolix (tediously wordy) fits. Austere, terse, and pithy describe the early style, not the later; cogent concerns persuasiveness, not length." },
  { id:"tc011", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:2,
    text:"The scientist's reputation for {1} was so firmly established that when she announced a startling result, colleagues assumed the data were sound rather than suspecting fraud.",
    options:[["mendacity","eccentricity","ambition","probity","diffidence"]],
    answer:[3],
    explanation:"Colleagues trusted her data and did not suspect fraud, so her reputation must be for honesty: probity. Mendacity (lying) is the opposite. Ambition or eccentricity might, if anything, raise suspicion, and diffidence (shyness) has nothing to do with trustworthiness." },
  { id:"tc012", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:2,
    text:"The courtier's {1} manner (he agreed eagerly with every royal opinion, however absurd) earned him favor at court but contempt among his peers.",
    options:[["obsequious","truculent","aloof","candid","imperious"]],
    answer:[0],
    explanation:"Eagerly agreeing with every royal opinion is servile flattery: obsequious. Truculent (combative) and imperious (domineering) are the opposite; aloof suggests distance; candid suggests honesty, which would mean disagreeing with absurd opinions." },
  { id:"tc013", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:2,
    text:"Though the museum's director insisted that the restructuring was merely administrative, critics suspected the explanation was {1}, a pretext for dismissing curators who had opposed her.",
    options:[["exhaustive","cogent","specious","ingenuous","forthright"]],
    answer:[2],
    explanation:"Critics thought the explanation was a \"pretext,\" plausible on its surface but false: specious. Cogent and forthright would mean the critics believed her; ingenuous means naive or innocent, which does not match a deliberate pretext; exhaustive concerns thoroughness." },
  { id:"tc014", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:2,
    text:"The economist's forecasts, once dismissed as hopelessly {1}, now seem prescient: the downturn she predicted arrived almost exactly on schedule.",
    options:[["alarmist","measured","sanguine","conventional","orthodox"]],
    answer:[0],
    explanation:"She predicted a downturn, and her forecasts were \"dismissed\" before proving \"prescient,\" so critics must have thought her needlessly gloomy: alarmist. Sanguine (optimistic) is the reverse of a downturn prediction; measured, conventional, and orthodox are not grounds for dismissing a forecast as \"hopelessly\" wrong." },
  { id:"tc015", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:2,
    text:"Biologists once regarded the trait as {1}, a mere byproduct of other adaptations, but new studies suggest it plays a central role in mate selection.",
    options:[["vital","adaptive","heritable","conspicuous","incidental"]],
    answer:[4],
    explanation:"The appositive \"a mere byproduct\" defines the blank, and \"but... central role\" confirms the contrast: incidental. Vital and adaptive describe the new view, not the old; heritable and conspicuous do not match \"mere byproduct.\"" },
  { id:"tc016", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:2,
    text:"The playwright was famously {1} about his methods; in fifty years of interviews he revealed almost nothing about how he wrote.",
    options:[["reticent","voluble","forthcoming","boastful","indecisive"]],
    answer:[0],
    explanation:"Revealing \"almost nothing\" over fifty years shows he was reticent (reluctant to disclose). Voluble, forthcoming, and boastful all imply talking freely about his methods; indecisive is about making decisions, not disclosure." },
  { id:"tc017", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:2,
    text:"Her colleagues mistook the senator's {1} for indifference, but in fact her calm reflected long experience rather than a lack of concern.",
    options:[["fervor","equanimity","petulance","irascibility","exuberance"]],
    answer:[1],
    explanation:"The quality was mistaken for indifference and is restated as \"her calm,\" so it means composure: equanimity. Fervor and exuberance could never be confused with indifference; petulance and irascibility are forms of irritability, not calm." },
  { id:"tc018", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:2,
    text:"Whereas his predecessor had squandered the foundation's endowment on lavish galas, the new director was almost {1}, scrutinizing every expenditure, however trivial.",
    options:[["profligate","munificent","parsimonious","prodigal","magnanimous"]],
    answer:[2],
    explanation:"\"Whereas\" contrasts the new director with a predecessor who \"squandered\" money, and he scrutinizes every expense, so he is extremely stingy: parsimonious. Profligate and prodigal describe the predecessor; munificent and magnanimous suggest generosity." },
  { id:"tc019", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:3,
    text:"The reviewer's praise was so heavily {1} that readers could be forgiven for concluding she had disliked the book: every compliment came hedged with a reservation.",
    options:[["effusive","unstinting","sincere","qualified","belated"]],
    answer:[3],
    explanation:"Here \"qualified\" carries its secondary sense of limited or hedged; \"every compliment came hedged with a reservation\" defines it exactly. Effusive and unstinting would make readers think she loved the book; sincere does not explain the confusion; belated concerns timing." },
  { id:"tc020", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:3,
    text:"Critics who expected the director's adaptation to be {1} were surprised to find that, far from slavishly reproducing the novel, it rearranged the plot and invented several characters.",
    options:[["iconoclastic","heterodox","inchoate","reverential","idiosyncratic"]],
    answer:[3],
    explanation:"The critics were \"surprised\" to find it was not a slavish reproduction, so they had expected a faithful, deferential treatment: reverential. Iconoclastic, heterodox, and idiosyncratic describe what the film actually turned out to be, so they would remove the surprise; inchoate means unformed." },
  { id:"tc021", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:3,
    text:"That the essay's central argument is {1} does not make it uninteresting; indeed, the very familiarity of its claims lets the author's stylistic inventiveness stand out all the more.",
    options:[["abstruse","novel","pedestrian","incendiary","convoluted"]],
    answer:[2],
    explanation:"\"The very familiarity of its claims\" tells us the argument is commonplace, which is the secondary meaning of pedestrian. Novel is the opposite of familiar; abstruse and convoluted mean hard to follow, and incendiary means inflammatory, none of which matches familiarity." },
  { id:"tc022", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:3,
    text:"Although her interest in the project never {1}, the composer's progress on it slowed to a crawl as her teaching duties multiplied.",
    options:[["intensified","materialized","crystallized","resurfaced","flagged"]],
    answer:[4],
    explanation:"\"Although\" contrasts her interest with the slowing progress: the work slowed, yet her interest did not decline. Flagged (weakened) fits. Intensified would make the contrast pointless; materialized, crystallized, and resurfaced would imply she was never interested at all, which does not fit the contrast." },
  { id:"tc023", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:3,
    text:"The senator's apology was so {1} that it seemed less an admission of fault than a further act of defiance.",
    options:[["contrite","abject","heartfelt","grudging","effusive"]],
    answer:[3],
    explanation:"An apology that reads as defiance must have been given reluctantly: grudging. Contrite, abject, and heartfelt describe a sincere, humble apology, the opposite of defiance; effusive (gushing) also suggests eagerness rather than resistance." },
  { id:"tc024", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:3,
    text:"Even the treatise's admirers concede that its arguments are sometimes {1}: the author so delights in fine distinctions that he occasionally loses sight of the larger question those distinctions were meant to illuminate.",
    options:[["cursory","pedantic","sweeping","facile","platitudinous"]],
    answer:[1],
    explanation:"Excessive delight in fine distinctions at the expense of the larger point is the mark of pedantry: pedantic. Cursory, sweeping, and facile suggest too little attention to detail, the opposite fault; platitudinous means full of clichés." },
  { id:"tc025", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:3,
    text:"The new theory's appeal lies partly in its {1}: by explaining a host of disparate phenomena with a single mechanism, it satisfies scientists' longstanding preference for economy of explanation.",
    options:[["novelty","complexity","profligacy","ambiguity","parsimony"]],
    answer:[4],
    explanation:"\"A single mechanism\" and \"economy of explanation\" point to parsimony in its scientific sense: explaining much with few assumptions. Complexity and profligacy are its opposites; novelty is not what \"economy\" describes; ambiguity is not an appeal." },
  { id:"tc026", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:3,
    text:"The composer's late quartets, long regarded as the {1} efforts of a declining talent, are now heard as deliberate experiments whose apparent incoherence conceals a rigorous design.",
    options:[["fastidious","desultory","meticulous","incisive","polished"]],
    answer:[1],
    explanation:"The old view is contrasted with \"deliberate experiments\" and \"rigorous design,\" and is linked to \"apparent incoherence\" and \"declining talent,\" so the blank means aimless and unmethodical: desultory. Fastidious, meticulous, polished, and incisive express the new, positive view, not the old dismissive one." },

  // ---------- Two-blank items ----------
  { id:"tc027", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:1,
    text:"The professor was known for her {1} lectures, which rambled from topic to topic; her published work, by contrast, was strikingly {2}.",
    options:[["desultory","incisive","succinct"],["diffuse","focused","meandering"]],
    answer:[0,1],
    explanation:"Lectures that \"rambled from topic to topic\" are desultory. \"By contrast\" requires the published work to be the opposite: focused. Diffuse and meandering would repeat the description of the lectures; incisive and succinct do not describe rambling." },
  { id:"tc028", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:1,
    text:"The explorer's mood shifted markedly over the course of the journey: initially {1} about the expedition's chances, he grew {2} as supplies dwindled and the weather worsened.",
    options:[["despairing","sanguine","dejected"],["confident","complacent","despondent"]],
    answer:[1,2],
    explanation:"\"Shifted markedly\" requires the two moods to contrast; worsening conditions would make him despondent, so at first he must have been hopeful: sanguine. Despairing and dejected leave no shift in mood. Confident and complacent do not fit dwindling supplies and worsening weather." },
  { id:"tc029", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:1,
    text:"The mayor's speech was {1}, filled with stale phrases that the audience had heard countless times; few listeners were {2} by it.",
    options:[["original","provocative","hackneyed"],["bored","moved","alienated"]],
    answer:[2,1],
    explanation:"\"Stale phrases... heard countless times\" defines hackneyed. Such a speech would leave listeners unaffected, so few were moved. \"Few were bored\" contradicts the stale content, and nothing suggests the speech was offensive enough to alienate." },
  { id:"tc030", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:1,
    text:"Unlike her {1} brother, who would chat with anyone, the poet was {2} and rarely spoke at gatherings.",
    options:[["loquacious","taciturn","morose"],["voluble","gregarious","taciturn"]],
    answer:[0,2],
    explanation:"The brother \"would chat with anyone\": loquacious. \"Unlike\" signals the poet is the opposite, and \"rarely spoke\" defines taciturn. Voluble and gregarious describe the brother, not the poet; morose means gloomy, not talkative." },
  { id:"tc031", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:1,
    text:"The committee's review of the proposal was so {1} that it overlooked several obvious errors; a more {2} examination would have caught them at once.",
    options:[["exhaustive","cursory","hostile"],["superficial","hasty","thorough"]],
    answer:[1,2],
    explanation:"Overlooking obvious errors implies a hasty, superficial review: cursory. The errors would have been caught by the opposite: a thorough examination. Exhaustive contradicts the missed errors; superficial and hasty repeat the problem rather than fixing it." },
  { id:"tc032", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:1,
    text:"The medicine did not cure the disease, but it did {1} its symptoms, making the patients' suffering considerably less {2}.",
    options:[["aggravate","alleviate","disguise"],["acute","tolerable","mysterious"]],
    answer:[1,0],
    explanation:"\"Did not cure... but\" signals a partial benefit: it alleviated the symptoms, making suffering less acute (severe). Aggravate is a harm, not a benefit; \"less tolerable\" would reverse the meaning; disguise and mysterious do not fit the logic of relief." },
  { id:"tc033", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:1,
    text:"Admirers {1} the philanthropist for her generosity, but her critics {2} her as a publicity seeker who gave only when cameras were present.",
    options:[["vilified","ignored","extolled"],["lauded","denigrated","emulated"]],
    answer:[2,1],
    explanation:"Admirers praising her \"for her generosity\" calls for extolled. \"But\" sets up the critics' opposite view, attacking her as a publicity seeker: denigrated. Vilified belongs to critics, not admirers; lauded and emulated are positive and do not fit the critics." },
  { id:"tc034", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:2,
    text:"Although the treaty was hailed as a {1} breakthrough, its provisions proved so {2} that within a year both nations were ignoring them.",
    options:[["historic","minor","tentative"],["rigorous","unenforceable","durable"]],
    answer:[0,1],
    explanation:"\"Hailed as a... breakthrough\" requires a word of high praise: historic. \"Although\" sets up a contrast, and nations ignoring the provisions shows they were unenforceable. Rigorous and durable would not explain why they were ignored; minor and tentative do not fit \"hailed.\"" },
  { id:"tc035", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:2,
    text:"The witness's testimony was riddled with {1}; nonetheless, the jury found him {2}, perhaps because his evident nervousness seemed a sign of sincerity rather than deceit.",
    options:[["eloquence","inconsistencies","detail"],["mendacious","credible","evasive"]],
    answer:[1,1],
    explanation:"\"Riddled with\" implies something negative, and \"nonetheless\" signals that the jury reacted against expectation; the nervousness \"seemed a sign of sincerity,\" so the jury found him credible despite his inconsistencies. Mendacious and evasive contradict sincerity; eloquence and detail are not flaws." },
  { id:"tc036", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:2,
    text:"The movement began as an {1} collection of discontented intellectuals, but within a decade it had {2} into a disciplined political party with millions of members.",
    options:[["organized","entrenched","inchoate"],["dwindled","coalesced","fragmented"]],
    answer:[2,1],
    explanation:"\"But\" contrasts the beginning with \"a disciplined political party,\" so the movement began formless: inchoate. It then came together: coalesced. Organized and entrenched destroy the contrast; dwindled and fragmented conflict with \"millions of members\" and \"disciplined.\"" },
  { id:"tc037", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:2,
    text:"The biographer avoids both {1} and denigration: she neither glosses over her subject's failings nor {2} his genuine achievements.",
    options:[["hagiography","criticism","objectivity"],["exaggerates","catalogues","belittles"]],
    answer:[0,2],
    explanation:"The colon maps each extreme onto a clause. Glossing over failings is the fault of hagiography (worshipful biography), and denigration corresponds to belittling achievements. Criticism is close to denigration, so it does not supply the opposite extreme; avoiding objectivity is not a virtue; exaggerating achievements would be a hagiographer's fault, not a denigrator's." },
  { id:"tc038", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:2,
    text:"Investors who had once regarded the company's chief executive as {1}, a visionary willing to take risks others avoided, now describe her as merely {2}, a gambler whose luck ran out.",
    options:[["cautious","intrepid","craven"],["reckless","prudent","timorous"]],
    answer:[1,0],
    explanation:"\"A visionary willing to take risks\" defines intrepid (boldly fearless). \"Merely\" and \"a gambler\" give the negative version of the same trait: reckless. Cautious and craven contradict risk-taking; prudent and timorous do not describe a gambler." },
  { id:"tc039", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:2,
    text:"The sculptor's early work was {1}, crowded with ornament and allegorical figures; her mature style, by contrast, is so {2} that some pieces consist of nothing but a single polished stone.",
    options:[["austere","florid","understated"],["baroque","ornate","austere"]],
    answer:[1,2],
    explanation:"\"Crowded with ornament\" calls for florid. \"By contrast\" and \"nothing but a single polished stone\" call for austere. Austere and understated do not fit the first blank; baroque and ornate would make the second blank repeat the first rather than contrast with it." },
  { id:"tc040", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:2,
    text:"Because the new instrument is far more sensitive than its predecessors, astronomers expect it to {1} a debate that earlier observations were too {2} to settle.",
    options:[["resolve","prolong","ignite"],["definitive","numerous","imprecise"]],
    answer:[0,2],
    explanation:"Greater sensitivity lets the instrument settle the debate: resolve. Earlier, less sensitive observations failed because they were imprecise. Prolong and ignite do not follow from better data; definitive observations would have settled the debate, and \"too numerous\" makes no sense." },
  { id:"tc041", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:2,
    text:"The diplomat had a gift for {1}: asked a direct question, she would produce an answer so carefully {2} that both sides could claim she had endorsed their position.",
    options:[["candor","equivocation","bluntness"],["hedged","pointed","incriminating"]],
    answer:[1,0],
    explanation:"An answer that lets both sides claim her endorsement is deliberately ambiguous: equivocation, produced by carefully hedged phrasing. Candor and bluntness would commit her to one side; pointed and incriminating answers would not satisfy both sides." },
  { id:"tc042", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:3,
    text:"Scholars have tended to {1} the poet's debt to popular ballads, treating it as a youthful affectation; yet the ballad's rhythms are {2} in even her most austere late lyrics.",
    options:[["overstate","discount","celebrate"],["discernible","absent","parodied"]],
    answer:[1,0],
    explanation:"Treating the debt as a mere \"youthful affectation\" minimizes it: discount. \"Yet\" introduces the author's rebuttal, that the influence persists into her late work: the rhythms are discernible even there. Overstate and celebrate are the reverse of treating it as a mere affectation; absent would agree with the scholars, removing the \"yet\"; parodied introduces an unsupported idea." },
  { id:"tc043", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:3,
    text:"Though he cultivated a reputation for {1}, the critic was in fact deeply {2}: his supposedly fearless reviews invariably flattered whichever writer happened to hold the most influential post.",
    options:[["independence","sycophancy","diffidence"],["iconoclastic","servile","disinterested"]],
    answer:[0,1],
    explanation:"\"Supposedly fearless\" shows the reputation was for independence; \"in fact\" reverses it, and flattering the powerful reveals him to be servile. Sycophancy is the reality, not the reputation; diffidence does not match \"fearless\"; iconoclastic and disinterested match the false reputation, not the truth." },
  { id:"tc044", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:3,
    text:"Her refusal to {1} her criticism of the board was not mere stubbornness; she believed that softening the language would {2} the very problems the report was meant to expose.",
    options:[["publish","retract","temper"],["highlight","obscure","resolve"]],
    answer:[2,1],
    explanation:"The second clause restates the refusal as declining to soften the language, which is the secondary sense of temper (moderate). Softened language would obscure the problems the report exposes. Retract means withdraw entirely, not soften; publish makes no sense with \"refusal\"; highlight and resolve are not plausible results of softening." },
  { id:"tc045", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:3,
    text:"That the author's style is {1} should not be mistaken for a lack of ambition; the very plainness of her sentences, stripped of every flourish, is the product of {2} revision.",
    options:[["ornate","prosaic","turgid"],["desultory","assiduous","cursory"]],
    answer:[1,1],
    explanation:"\"The very plainness of her sentences\" identifies the style as prosaic (plain, unadorned). Writing stripped of every flourish, as a sign of ambition, must come from careful, persistent revision: assiduous. Ornate and turgid contradict plainness; desultory and cursory revision would suggest the lack of ambition the sentence denies." },
  { id:"tc046", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:3,
    text:"The economist's new book is less a {1} of prevailing theory than a refinement of it; readers expecting the iconoclasm of her earlier work may find it {2}.",
    options:[["corroboration","summary","repudiation"],["incendiary","tame","impenetrable"]],
    answer:[2,1],
    explanation:"\"Less a ___ than a refinement\" requires something more radical than refinement: repudiation. Readers who expected iconoclasm will find a mere refinement tame. Corroboration and summary are no more radical than refinement; incendiary describes the iconoclasm they will not find; impenetrable concerns difficulty, not boldness." },
  { id:"tc047", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:3,
    text:"Insofar as the committee's report merely {1} conclusions that had been reached years earlier, its authors' claims to have broken new ground seem {2}.",
    options:[["recapitulates","overturns","anticipates"],["warranted","modest","unfounded"]],
    answer:[0,2],
    explanation:"\"Merely\" signals that the report does nothing new: it recapitulates (restates) old conclusions. Claims of originality are therefore unfounded. Overturning old conclusions would be new ground; one cannot anticipate conclusions reached years earlier; warranted reverses the logic; modest does not describe a claim to have broken new ground." },
  { id:"tc048", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:3,
    text:"The general's reputation for {1} rests largely on a single famous retreat, which admirers interpret as prudence; his detractors, however, see in that same episode evidence of a {2} temperament.",
    options:[["temerity","caution","flamboyance"],["rash","magnanimous","pusillanimous"]],
    answer:[1,2],
    explanation:"A retreat that admirers call \"prudence\" builds a reputation for caution. \"However\" introduces the detractors' negative reading of the same retreat: cowardice, or a pusillanimous temperament. Temerity (recklessness) and rash contradict a retreat; flamboyance and magnanimous do not fit the episode." },

  // ---------- Three-blank items ----------
  { id:"tc049", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:1,
    text:"For decades, the island's ecosystem remained remarkably {1}, largely untouched by outside influences. The arrival of rats aboard trading ships, however, proved {2} for the native birds, whose populations {3} within a generation.",
    options:[["degraded","volatile","pristine"],["beneficial","calamitous","inconsequential"],["burgeoned","stabilized","plummeted"]],
    answer:[2,1,2],
    explanation:"\"Largely untouched\" defines pristine. \"However\" signals a change for the worse, so the rats were calamitous and the bird populations plummeted. Degraded and volatile contradict \"untouched\"; beneficial and inconsequential and burgeoned/stabilized would remove the contrast." },
  { id:"tc050", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:1,
    text:"The candidate's speeches were notably {1}, often running past two hours. Her advisers urged her to be more {2}, warning that audiences would grow {3} if she did not shorten them.",
    options:[["terse","verbose","inaudible"],["expansive","emphatic","concise"],["restive","enthralled","sympathetic"]],
    answer:[1,2,0],
    explanation:"Speeches \"running past two hours\" are verbose; advisers urging her to \"shorten them\" want her to be concise; the warning is that audiences would become impatient: restive. Terse and expansive reverse the logic; enthralled and sympathetic are not consequences one warns about." },
  { id:"tc051", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:1,
    text:"Most early reviewers {1} the novel, calling it confused and tedious. Yet within a generation critics had come to {2} it as a masterpiece, and today it is taught in nearly every survey course. The book's {3} reputation shows how unreliable first impressions can be.",
    options:[["praised","panned","ignored"],["dismiss","extol","parody"],["unchanging","diminished","transformed"]],
    answer:[1,1,2],
    explanation:"Calling the novel \"confused and tedious\" means reviewers panned it (ignored is impossible, since they commented). \"Yet\" and \"masterpiece\" call for extol. A reputation that went from scorn to acclaim is transformed, which is why first impressions proved unreliable; unchanging and diminished contradict the story." },
  { id:"tc052", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:1,
    text:"The scientist was {1} in her methods, repeating each experiment dozens of times. Her rival, by contrast, was notoriously {2}, publishing results after a single trial. Unsurprisingly, his findings were frequently {3} by later researchers.",
    options:[["eccentric","careless","meticulous"],["scrupulous","slapdash","reticent"],["refuted","confirmed","cited"]],
    answer:[2,1,0],
    explanation:"Repeating experiments dozens of times is meticulous; \"by contrast,\" the rival who published after one trial was slapdash; \"unsurprisingly,\" such hasty work was often refuted. Careless and scrupulous are on the wrong sides; confirmed would be surprising, and cited is neutral." },
  { id:"tc053", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:1,
    text:"At first the protest was small and {1}, attracting only a handful of students. As news of the arrests spread, however, it {2} rapidly, and within a week tens of thousands had joined. Officials who had initially {3} the demonstrations were forced to negotiate.",
    options:[["massive","obscure","celebrated"],["waned","stagnated","burgeoned"],["organized","dismissed","encouraged"]],
    answer:[1,2,1],
    explanation:"\"Small\" and \"only a handful\" pair with obscure; growth to tens of thousands means it burgeoned; officials \"forced to negotiate\" must earlier have dismissed the protest as unimportant. Massive and celebrated contradict \"small\"; waned and stagnated contradict the growth; officials would not have organized or encouraged a protest they were later forced to negotiate with." },
  { id:"tc054", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:1,
    text:"The old library's reading room was {1}, its walls covered in gilded carvings and painted ceilings. The new building, in keeping with modern taste, is {2}: white walls, plain furniture, and nothing to {3} the reader.",
    options:[["spartan","dilapidated","opulent"],["lavish","austere","garish"],["guide","illuminate","distract"]],
    answer:[2,1,2],
    explanation:"Gilded carvings and painted ceilings are opulent. White walls and plain furniture are austere. The minimalism is framed approvingly (\"in keeping with modern taste\"), so there is nothing to distract the reader. Spartan and lavish/garish are on the wrong sides; guide and illuminate do not follow from plainness." },
  { id:"tc055", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:2,
    text:"The composer's critics charged that her music was merely {1}, a clever pastiche of earlier styles. Her defenders countered that what looked like imitation was in fact {2}: she quoted older composers in order to comment on them, not to {3} them.",
    options:[["original","derivative","atonal"],["plagiarism","incompetence","critique"],["parody","interrogate","mimic"]],
    answer:[1,2,2],
    explanation:"\"A clever pastiche of earlier styles\" defines derivative. The defenders recast the imitation as commentary: critique. The final contrast is with mere copying: \"to comment on them, not to mimic them.\" Plagiarism is the critics' view, not the defenders'; parody and interrogate are forms of commentary and so cannot be what she avoided." },
  { id:"tc056", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:2,
    text:"Historians long portrayed the regional governor as a {1} administrator who enriched himself at public expense. Recently discovered ledgers, however, suggest a man of unusual {2}: he refused gifts, recorded every expense, and died nearly {3}.",
    options:[["scrupulous","venal","indolent"],["cupidity","indolence","probity"],["affluent","destitute","celebrated"]],
    answer:[1,2,1],
    explanation:"Enriching himself at public expense makes him venal (corrupt). \"However\" reverses this: refusing gifts shows probity (integrity). A man who never profited from office died nearly destitute. Cupidity (greed) fits the old portrait, not the new; affluent contradicts his honesty; celebrated is unsupported." },
  { id:"tc057", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:2,
    text:"The new mayor's plan was {1} in its ambitions, promising to eliminate homelessness within a single term. Even sympathetic observers considered it {2}, and when the budget was cut, the mayor quietly {3} the most expensive provisions.",
    options:[["modest","incremental","sweeping"],["pragmatic","quixotic","cynical"],["expanded","jettisoned","defended"]],
    answer:[2,1,1],
    explanation:"Promising to eliminate homelessness in one term is sweeping. \"Even sympathetic observers\" thought such idealism impractical: quixotic. After budget cuts, she \"quietly\" dropped the costly parts: jettisoned. Modest and incremental contradict the promise; pragmatic is the opposite of the criticism; expanded and defended do not fit \"quietly\" after cuts." },
  { id:"tc058", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:2,
    text:"Though the anthropologist's prose is {1}, studded with technical jargon and nested clauses, her central claim is {2}: that rituals persist because they are useful. Readers willing to {3} her style will find the argument surprisingly persuasive.",
    options:[["lucid","lyrical","abstruse"],["straightforward","convoluted","incoherent"],["emulate","overlook","praise"]],
    answer:[2,0,1],
    explanation:"Jargon and nested clauses make prose abstruse (hard to understand). \"Though\" contrasts this with a simple claim, and the claim given is indeed straightforward. Readers must overlook the difficult style to appreciate the argument. Lucid contradicts the jargon; convoluted and incoherent would remove the contrast; emulating or praising the style is not the point." },
  { id:"tc059", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:2,
    text:"Early accounts of the expedition were {1}, presenting its leader as a flawless hero. Later chroniclers, more {2}, noted his frequent miscalculations, though they too were reluctant to {3} the genuine courage he displayed.",
    options:[["scathing","hagiographic","dispassionate"],["reverent","credulous","skeptical"],["impugn","acknowledge","celebrate"]],
    answer:[1,2,0],
    explanation:"Presenting someone as a flawless hero is hagiographic. Chroniclers who noted his errors were more skeptical. \"Though they too\" signals that even they respected him: they were reluctant to impugn (cast doubt on) his courage. Reluctance to acknowledge or celebrate it would contradict \"genuine courage\" and the respectful \"too.\"" },
  { id:"tc060", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:2,
    text:"The negotiator's {1} manner (she rarely spoke, and never more than necessary) was often taken for {2}. Those who worked with her, however, knew that her silence was strategic: it {3} her counterparts into revealing more than they intended.",
    options:[["garrulous","bombastic","laconic"],["eloquence","timidity","candor"],["deterred","lured","bullied"]],
    answer:[2,1,1],
    explanation:"Rarely speaking and never more than necessary is laconic. \"However... strategic\" implies observers wrongly read the silence as weakness: timidity. Strategic silence lured counterparts into over-sharing. Garrulous and bombastic contradict silence; eloquence and candor are not misreadings of silence; deterred reverses the effect and bullied does not fit silence." },
  { id:"tc061", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:3,
    text:"It is tempting to read the philosopher's late essays as a {1} of his earlier positions, since they so often seem to contradict them. A closer reading, however, suggests that the late work {2} rather than abandons those positions: the core commitments survive, but hedged with exceptions that make them more {3}.",
    options:[["restatement","recantation","vindication"],["repudiates","reiterates","qualifies"],["dogmatic","defensible","sweeping"]],
    answer:[1,2,1],
    explanation:"Essays that \"seem to contradict\" earlier positions tempt readers to see a recantation. \"Rather than abandons\" plus \"hedged with exceptions\" defines qualifies (limits). Exceptions make claims narrower and thus more defensible. Restatement and reiterates ignore the apparent contradiction; repudiates means abandons; dogmatic and sweeping are the opposite of hedged." },
  { id:"tc062", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:3,
    text:"The committee's final report was hardly the {1} that its critics had feared. Far from whitewashing the agency's failures, it catalogued them in {2} detail; if anything, the report's flaw was its {3}, since its authors seemed so eager to assign blame that they ignored the extenuating circumstances under which officials had worked.",
    options:[["indictment","jeremiad","exoneration"],["cursory","unsparing","euphemistic"],["leniency","brevity","severity"]],
    answer:[2,1,2],
    explanation:"Critics feared a whitewash, an exoneration; \"far from whitewashing\" shows the report detailed failures unsparingly. Its flaw was the opposite excess: severity, being so eager to blame that it ignored mitigating circumstances. Indictment and jeremiad describe what the report actually was; cursory and euphemistic match the feared whitewash; leniency reverses the flaw." },
  { id:"tc063", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:3,
    text:"Although the novelist was celebrated in her lifetime for her {1}, contemporary readers are more likely to be struck by her restraint. The emotional climaxes that so moved her first audience are, on the page, curiously {2}; her characters register catastrophe with a shrug, and the narration {3} comment.",
    options:[["reticence","pathos","concision"],["overwrought","maudlin","muted"],["eschews","indulges in","invites"]],
    answer:[1,2,0],
    explanation:"\"Although\" contrasts the past reputation with today's impression of restraint, so she was celebrated for emotional power: pathos. The climaxes are \"curiously\" muted, consistent with characters who shrug, and the narration eschews (avoids) comment. Reticence and concision are forms of restraint and remove the contrast; overwrought and maudlin contradict restraint; indulging in or inviting comment contradicts it as well." },
  { id:"tc064", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:3,
    text:"The museum's new director has been praised for making its collections more {1}, yet some curators worry that her efforts to attract a broad audience have {2} the institution's scholarly mission. Their concern is not that popular exhibitions are inherently {3}, but that the resources devoted to them have come at the expense of research.",
    options:[["esoteric","valuable","accessible"],["advanced","compromised","clarified"],["lucrative","frivolous","edifying"]],
    answer:[2,1,1],
    explanation:"Attracting a broad audience means making collections accessible. \"Yet... worry\" introduces harm to the scholarly mission: compromised. The final sentence clarifies the worry is about resources, not that popular shows are inherently frivolous (lacking seriousness). Esoteric is the opposite of broad appeal; advanced and clarified are not worries; \"not that they are inherently edifying\" or \"lucrative\" does not state a plausible objection being set aside." },
  { id:"tc065", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:3,
    text:"Few scientists today would defend the theory in its original form, yet it would be a mistake to regard it as merely {1}. Its central insight (that organisms actively shape the environments to which they adapt) was {2} rather than refuted by subsequent research, which retained the idea while discarding the {3} mechanism its originator proposed to explain it.",
    options:[["prescient","influential","obsolete"],["discredited","absorbed","anticipated"],["corroborated","empirical","erroneous"]],
    answer:[2,1,2],
    explanation:"\"Few would defend it... yet it would be a mistake\" means it should not be dismissed as merely obsolete. \"Rather than refuted\" and \"retained the idea\" call for absorbed. A mechanism that was discarded and that no one now defends was erroneous. Prescient and influential are not dismissive; discredited means refuted; anticipated reverses chronology; a corroborated mechanism would not be discarded." },
  { id:"tc066", type:"tc", topic:"tc", difficulty:3,
    text:"The diarist's entries are so {1} that historians once dismissed them as useless: a typical day receives a single line, often noting only the weather. Yet precisely this {2} has proven valuable, for climatologists reconstructing the region's past have found in her {3} weather records a dataset unmatched by any official source.",
    options:[["effusive","terse","fanciful"],["embellishment","candor","brevity"],["sporadic","florid","assiduous"]],
    answer:[1,2,2],
    explanation:"\"A single line\" per day is terse; \"precisely this\" refers back to that brevity. Records that form an unmatched dataset must have been kept diligently and consistently: assiduous. Effusive and embellishment contradict single-line entries; sporadic records would not form an unmatched dataset; florid contradicts terse." }
];

window.VERBAL_SE = [
  { id:"se001", type:"se", topic:"se", difficulty:1,
    text:"Though the young actor seemed {1} in interviews, saying little and avoiding eye contact, he was a commanding presence on stage.",
    options:["garrulous","reticent","loquacious","arrogant","reserved","charismatic"],
    answer:[1,4],
    explanation:"\"Saying little and avoiding eye contact,\" contrasted (\"Though\") with a commanding stage presence, calls for reticent and reserved. Garrulous and loquacious are a synonym pair meaning talkative, the opposite of the clue. Charismatic contradicts the contrast; arrogant does not fit avoiding eye contact." },
  { id:"se002", type:"se", topic:"se", difficulty:1,
    text:"The CEO's {1} remarks at the shareholders' meeting, which lasted less than a minute, left investors hungry for details.",
    options:["prolix","laconic","candid","verbose","terse","heartening"],
    answer:[1,4],
    explanation:"Remarks lasting under a minute that leave listeners wanting details are brief: laconic and terse. Prolix and verbose form a tempting pair but mean wordy. Candid and heartening could describe remarks, but neither has a partner and neither explains why investors wanted more details." },
  { id:"se003", type:"se", topic:"se", difficulty:1,
    text:"The craze for the toy proved {1}: within a single season, stores that had sold out could not give it away.",
    options:["evanescent","lucrative","durable","profitable","fleeting","widespread"],
    answer:[0,4],
    explanation:"The colon explains that the craze vanished within a season, so it was short-lived: evanescent and fleeting. Lucrative and profitable form a pair, but stores unable to give the toy away contradicts lasting profit. Durable is the opposite; widespread has no partner." },
  { id:"se004", type:"se", topic:"se", difficulty:1,
    text:"Exasperated by her son's {1} refusal to do his homework, the mother finally called his teacher.",
    options:["docile","obstinate","compliant","sporadic","innocent","recalcitrant"],
    answer:[1,5],
    explanation:"A refusal that exasperates is stubborn: obstinate and recalcitrant. Docile and compliant are a synonym pair but mean obedient, which contradicts \"refusal.\" Sporadic and innocent do not fit an exasperating refusal." },
  { id:"se005", type:"se", topic:"se", difficulty:1,
    text:"The reviewer {1} the film as a shallow, cynical exercise in marketing.",
    options:["lauded","disparaged","extolled","summarized","produced","denigrated"],
    answer:[1,5],
    explanation:"Calling the film \"shallow\" and \"cynical\" is harsh criticism: disparaged and denigrated. Lauded and extolled are a pair meaning praised, the opposite. Summarized and produced lack partners and do not fit the evaluative tone." },
  { id:"se006", type:"se", topic:"se", difficulty:1,
    text:"Because the old bridge was considered unsafe, engineers worked {1} to complete its replacement before winter.",
    options:["lackadaisically","secretly","assiduously","desultorily","diligently","reluctantly"],
    answer:[2,4],
    explanation:"Urgency (\"unsafe,\" \"before winter\") implies hard, careful work: assiduously and diligently. Lackadaisically and desultorily form a pair meaning half-heartedly, which contradicts the urgency. Secretly and reluctantly have no partners." },
  { id:"se007", type:"se", topic:"se", difficulty:1,
    text:"The town's {1} donor, who funded the library, the hospital, and the park, asked that her name appear on none of them.",
    options:["munificent","parsimonious","miserly","wealthy","anonymous","generous"],
    answer:[0,5],
    explanation:"Funding three major institutions shows great generosity: munificent and generous. Parsimonious and miserly are a pair meaning stingy, the opposite. Wealthy fits but has no synonym among the choices; anonymous is redundant with the second clause and also lacks a partner." },
  { id:"se008", type:"se", topic:"se", difficulty:1,
    text:"The speaker's {1} jokes, which the audience had heard many times before, drew only polite chuckles.",
    options:["fresh","trite","novel","offensive","hackneyed","witty"],
    answer:[1,4],
    explanation:"Jokes \"heard many times before\" are overused: trite and hackneyed. Fresh and novel form a pair meaning new, the opposite. Witty contradicts \"only polite chuckles\"; offensive has no partner and is unsupported." },
  { id:"se009", type:"se", topic:"se", difficulty:1,
    text:"Scientists were {1} about the drug's prospects after early trials showed dramatic improvements in patients.",
    options:["dubious","skeptical","sanguine","indifferent","secretive","optimistic"],
    answer:[2,5],
    explanation:"Dramatic improvements would make scientists hopeful: sanguine and optimistic. Dubious and skeptical form a pair but contradict the positive evidence. Indifferent and secretive have no partners." },
  { id:"se010", type:"se", topic:"se", difficulty:1,
    text:"Despite the coach's attempts to {1} the players' fears, the team remained anxious before the final.",
    options:["exacerbate","allay","aggravate","assuage","ignore","exploit"],
    answer:[1,3],
    explanation:"\"Despite\" signals the attempts failed, and the team \"remained anxious,\" so the coach tried to calm their fears: allay and assuage. Exacerbate and aggravate form a pair meaning worsen, which would make \"despite\" illogical. Ignore and exploit lack partners." },
  { id:"se011", type:"se", topic:"se", difficulty:1,
    text:"Her {1} manner at the party, chatting with everyone from the host to the catering staff, made her the evening's most memorable guest.",
    options:["taciturn","formal","gregarious","reserved","sociable","elegant"],
    answer:[2,4],
    explanation:"Chatting with everyone describes a friendly, outgoing manner: gregarious and sociable. Taciturn and reserved form a pair meaning quiet, the opposite. Elegant could describe a memorable guest but has no partner and does not match the chatting clue; formal likewise." },
  { id:"se012", type:"se", topic:"se", difficulty:1,
    text:"Investigators quickly realized that the documents were {1}, having been forged by the dealer himself.",
    options:["authentic","spurious","genuine","valuable","counterfeit","ancient"],
    answer:[1,4],
    explanation:"Forged documents are fake: spurious and counterfeit. Authentic and genuine form a pair meaning real, contradicting \"forged.\" Valuable and ancient have no partners and are not supported." },
  { id:"se013", type:"se", topic:"se", difficulty:1,
    text:"The senator was known for her {1} with public funds; she flew economy class and refused to redecorate her office.",
    options:["extravagance","frugality","profligacy","transparency","generosity","thrift"],
    answer:[1,5],
    explanation:"Flying economy and declining to redecorate show careful spending: frugality and thrift. Extravagance and profligacy form a pair meaning wastefulness, the opposite. Transparency could loosely fit but has no partner and is not what the examples illustrate." },
  { id:"se014", type:"se", topic:"se", difficulty:1,
    text:"The {1} tourist believed every story the guide told, no matter how far-fetched.",
    options:["skeptical","weary","credulous","cynical","wealthy","gullible"],
    answer:[2,5],
    explanation:"Believing far-fetched stories indicates someone easily fooled: credulous and gullible. Skeptical and cynical are near-synonyms meaning distrustful, the opposite. Weary and wealthy are unrelated to belief." },
  { id:"se015", type:"se", topic:"se", difficulty:1,
    text:"The once-mighty empire {1} over three centuries, losing province after province to its rivals.",
    options:["flourished","expanded","declined","rebelled","waned","persisted"],
    answer:[2,4],
    explanation:"\"Once-mighty\" and \"losing province after province\" describe decline: declined and waned. Flourished and expanded form a pair but contradict the losses. Persisted and rebelled have no partners." },
  { id:"se016", type:"se", topic:"se", difficulty:1,
    text:"The novel's plot is {1}, wandering from one minor character to another without ever building momentum.",
    options:["rambling","taut","gripping","meandering","predictable","melancholy"],
    answer:[0,3],
    explanation:"\"Wandering\" without momentum calls for rambling and meandering. Taut and gripping form a loose pair but contradict the lack of momentum. Predictable and melancholy have no partners and are not suggested by the clue." },
  { id:"se017", type:"se", topic:"se", difficulty:2,
    text:"The professor's lectures were notoriously {1}, so packed with tangents and qualifications that students struggled to identify the main point.",
    options:["pithy","succinct","prolix","abstruse","verbose","engaging"],
    answer:[2,4],
    explanation:"Lectures packed with tangents are long-winded: prolix and verbose. Pithy and succinct form a pair meaning concise, the opposite. Abstruse (hard to understand) is tempting since students struggled, but it describes difficulty of content, not wordiness, and has no partner." },
  { id:"se018", type:"se", topic:"se", difficulty:2,
    text:"Far from being {1}, the new manager made decisions only after weeks of consultation and analysis.",
    options:["deliberate","methodical","impetuous","indecisive","rash","popular"],
    answer:[2,4],
    explanation:"\"Far from\" requires the opposite of slow, careful decision-making: impetuous and rash. Deliberate and methodical form a pair but describe what she actually did, so they make \"far from\" illogical. Indecisive and popular have no partners." },
  { id:"se019", type:"se", topic:"se", difficulty:2,
    text:"The ambassador's {1} response neither confirmed nor denied the rumors of a secret agreement.",
    options:["candid","forthright","equivocal","belligerent","tardy","ambiguous"],
    answer:[2,5],
    explanation:"A response that \"neither confirmed nor denied\" is noncommittal: equivocal and ambiguous. Candid and forthright are a pair meaning frank, which contradicts the evasion. Belligerent and tardy have no partners." },
  { id:"se020", type:"se", topic:"se", difficulty:2,
    text:"Critics accused the columnist of {1}, noting that he praised every politician who granted him an interview.",
    options:["sycophancy","impartiality","pedantry","obsequiousness","objectivity","venality"],
    answer:[0,3],
    explanation:"Praising anyone who grants access is servile flattery: sycophancy and obsequiousness. Impartiality and objectivity form a pair but contradict the accusation. Venality (corruption by bribery) is tempting, but nothing indicates he was paid, and it has no partner." },
  { id:"se021", type:"se", topic:"se", difficulty:2,
    text:"Although the initial symptoms were mild, the infection could {1} rapidly if left untreated.",
    options:["abate","worsen","subside","spread","heal","deteriorate"],
    answer:[1,5],
    explanation:"\"Although... mild\" sets up a contrast: the condition could become serious, so worsen and deteriorate. Abate and subside form a pair meaning lessen, which removes the contrast. Spread fits plausibly but has no partner; heal contradicts \"if left untreated.\"" },
  { id:"se022", type:"se", topic:"se", difficulty:2,
    text:"The new evidence {1} the defendant's alibi, making his conviction far less likely.",
    options:["corroborated","undermined","impugned","fabricated","ignored","substantiated"],
    answer:[0,5],
    explanation:"Evidence that makes conviction less likely supports the alibi: corroborated and substantiated. Undermined and impugned form a pair, but weakening the alibi would make conviction more likely. Fabricated and ignored lack partners." },
  { id:"se023", type:"se", topic:"se", difficulty:2,
    text:"The painter's {1} temperament made her difficult to work with: she would be cheerful one moment and furious the next.",
    options:["phlegmatic","placid","mercurial","morose","capricious","artistic"],
    answer:[2,4],
    explanation:"Rapid shifts from cheerful to furious mark an unpredictable temperament: mercurial and capricious. Phlegmatic and placid form a pair meaning calm, the opposite. Morose (gloomy) does not capture the swings; artistic has no partner." },
  { id:"se024", type:"se", topic:"se", difficulty:2,
    text:"The treaty did little to {1} tensions between the two countries, which continued to skirmish along the border.",
    options:["exacerbate","mitigate","inflame","alleviate","document","reveal"],
    answer:[1,3],
    explanation:"Continued skirmishing shows the treaty failed to reduce tensions: mitigate and alleviate. Exacerbate and inflame form a pair, but \"did little to inflame\" would not explain ongoing fighting. Document and reveal have no partners." },
  { id:"se025", type:"se", topic:"se", difficulty:2,
    text:"The scholar's {1} approach to the manuscripts (examining every watermark and ink stain) yielded discoveries her hastier colleagues had missed.",
    options:["perfunctory","innovative","scrupulous","cursory","controversial","meticulous"],
    answer:[2,5],
    explanation:"Examining every watermark and ink stain, in contrast to \"hastier colleagues,\" is painstaking: scrupulous and meticulous. Perfunctory and cursory form a pair meaning superficial, which describes the colleagues. Innovative and controversial lack partners." },
  { id:"se026", type:"se", topic:"se", difficulty:2,
    text:"The poet's early verse, with its {1} imagery and elaborate rhymes, gave way in later years to a spare, almost conversational style.",
    options:["austere","stark","vivid","obscure","florid","ornate"],
    answer:[4,5],
    explanation:"Paired with \"elaborate rhymes\" and contrasted with the later \"spare\" style, the imagery must be highly decorated: florid and ornate. Austere and stark form a pair describing the later style. Vivid fits loosely but has no partner and does not capture the contrast with spareness." },
  { id:"se027", type:"se", topic:"se", difficulty:2,
    text:"The general's {1} conduct during the siege (he fled the city in disguise while his soldiers fought on) earned him lasting disgrace.",
    options:["intrepid","cunning","craven","valiant","pusillanimous","dishonest"],
    answer:[2,4],
    explanation:"Fleeing while his soldiers fought is cowardly: craven and pusillanimous. Intrepid and valiant form a pair meaning brave, the opposite. Cunning and dishonest might describe the disguise but have no partner and miss the central point of cowardice." },
  { id:"se028", type:"se", topic:"se", difficulty:2,
    text:"Initially a minor movement, the protest {1} into a national campaign within months.",
    options:["dwindled","burgeoned","waned","evolved","fragmented","mushroomed"],
    answer:[1,5],
    explanation:"Going from minor to national within months is rapid growth: burgeoned and mushroomed. Dwindled and waned form a pair meaning shrank. Evolved fits grammatically but lacks the sense of rapid growth and has no partner; fragmented contradicts a unified national campaign." },
  { id:"se029", type:"se", topic:"se", difficulty:2,
    text:"The investigation revealed a culture of {1} within the agency, where officials routinely accepted bribes in exchange for permits.",
    options:["probity","integrity","venality","inefficiency","corruption","secrecy"],
    answer:[2,4],
    explanation:"Routinely accepting bribes is venality, or corruption. Probity and integrity form a pair meaning honesty, the opposite. Inefficiency and secrecy might accompany bribery but are not what the clue defines, and neither has a partner." },
  { id:"se030", type:"se", topic:"se", difficulty:2,
    text:"Reviewers found the sequel {1}, lacking the wit and invention that had made the original so delightful.",
    options:["insipid","incisive","vapid","piquant","lengthy","expensive"],
    answer:[0,2],
    explanation:"A work lacking wit and invention is dull and flavorless: insipid and vapid. Incisive and piquant roughly pair as sharp or stimulating, the opposite. Lengthy and expensive are irrelevant to wit and have no partners." },
  { id:"se031", type:"se", topic:"se", difficulty:2,
    text:"The clerk gave the documents only a {1} glance before stamping them approved.",
    options:["thorough","exhaustive","cursory","suspicious","hostile","perfunctory"],
    answer:[2,5],
    explanation:"\"Only a ___ glance\" before automatic approval calls for a hasty, superficial look: cursory and perfunctory. Thorough and exhaustive form a pair contradicting \"only\" and \"glance.\" Suspicious and hostile do not fit stamping the documents approved." },
  { id:"se032", type:"se", topic:"se", difficulty:2,
    text:"The memoir's {1} tone (its author never raises her voice, even when describing grave injustices) makes its accusations all the more devastating.",
    options:["strident","shrill","bitter","nostalgic","measured","restrained"],
    answer:[4,5],
    explanation:"\"Never raises her voice\" defines a calm, controlled tone: measured and restrained. Strident and shrill form a pair meaning harsh and loud, the opposite. Bitter and nostalgic lack partners and contradict the parenthetical." },
  { id:"se033", type:"se", topic:"se", difficulty:3,
    text:"Though the minister's resignation letter was ostensibly an apology, its {1} tone suggested that she regarded herself as the injured party.",
    options:["contrite","penitent","aggrieved","conciliatory","laconic","indignant"],
    answer:[2,5],
    explanation:"\"Ostensibly\" signals that the real tone differs from an apology; regarding herself as \"the injured party\" implies resentment: aggrieved and indignant. Contrite and penitent form a pair matching the apology she only appeared to give. Conciliatory also fits the surface apology; laconic has no partner." },
  { id:"se034", type:"se", topic:"se", difficulty:3,
    text:"The essay's argument is more {1} than its breezy style suggests: beneath the jokes lies a careful, closely reasoned case.",
    options:["facile","rigorous","glib","entertaining","cogent","controversial"],
    answer:[1,4],
    explanation:"\"Careful, closely reasoned\" defines the blank, contrasted with a \"breezy style\": rigorous and cogent. Facile and glib form a pair, but they describe what the breezy style misleadingly suggests. Entertaining describes the style, not the argument; controversial has no partner." },
  { id:"se035", type:"se", topic:"se", difficulty:3,
    text:"A thoroughly {1} thinker, the historian accepts no claim simply because generations of scholars have repeated it.",
    options:["iconoclastic","orthodox","prolific","heterodox","conventional","pedantic"],
    answer:[0,3],
    explanation:"Refusing to accept received wisdom marks an unorthodox thinker: iconoclastic and heterodox. Orthodox and conventional form a pair describing the opposite. Prolific and pedantic lack partners and are not suggested by the clue." },
  { id:"se036", type:"se", topic:"se", difficulty:3,
    text:"The agency's response to the crisis was {1} at best; officials went through the motions of an investigation but showed little interest in its findings.",
    options:["assiduous","perfunctory","sedulous","half-hearted","ruthless","precipitous"],
    answer:[1,3],
    explanation:"\"Went through the motions\" with \"little interest\" defines perfunctory and half-hearted. Assiduous and sedulous form a pair meaning diligent, the opposite. Ruthless and precipitous (overly hasty) do not match going through the motions and lack partners." },
  { id:"se037", type:"se", topic:"se", difficulty:3,
    text:"Her colleagues were surprised by the {1} of her final report, since her earlier memos had been notoriously long-winded.",
    options:["prolixity","accuracy","concision","verbosity","brevity","tardiness"],
    answer:[2,4],
    explanation:"Surprise relative to \"long-winded\" earlier memos implies shortness: concision and brevity. Prolixity and verbosity form a pair, but they match the earlier memos and would not be surprising. Accuracy fits grammatically but has no partner and no link to long-windedness." },
  { id:"se038", type:"se", topic:"se", difficulty:3,
    text:"Because the evidence for the hypothesis remains {1}, most researchers treat it as a promising lead rather than an established fact.",
    options:["compelling","inconclusive","incontrovertible","abundant","fabricated","equivocal"],
    answer:[1,5],
    explanation:"Treating something as a lead rather than a fact means the evidence does not settle the question: inconclusive and equivocal. Compelling and incontrovertible form a loose pair suggesting strong evidence, which would make it an established fact. Fabricated would make it no lead at all; abundant lacks a partner." },
  { id:"se039", type:"se", topic:"se", difficulty:3,
    text:"The candidate's {1} on the issue (supporting the measure one week, opposing it the next) cost her the trust of both camps.",
    options:["intransigence","obduracy","silence","vacillation","eloquence","wavering"],
    answer:[3,5],
    explanation:"Switching positions week to week is vacillation, or wavering. Intransigence and obduracy form a pair meaning stubborn inflexibility, the opposite. Silence contradicts taking public positions; eloquence lacks a partner." },
  { id:"se040", type:"se", topic:"se", difficulty:3,
    text:"The biologist's findings, though modest in scope, have proven remarkably {1}, shaping the direction of research for a generation.",
    options:["ephemeral","evanescent","seminal","controversial","influential","replicable"],
    answer:[2,4],
    explanation:"\"Shaping the direction of research for a generation\" defines seminal and influential. Ephemeral and evanescent form a pair meaning short-lived, contradicting \"for a generation.\" Replicable and controversial lack partners and do not match the clue." },
  { id:"se041", type:"se", topic:"se", difficulty:3,
    text:"Some readers find the novelist's {1} irritating, but her refusal to tie up loose ends is precisely what lends her fiction its unsettling power.",
    options:["ambiguity","didacticism","sententiousness","indeterminacy","verbosity","sentimentality"],
    answer:[0,3],
    explanation:"The quality is restated as a \"refusal to tie up loose ends\": ambiguity and indeterminacy. Didacticism and sententiousness form a pair meaning moralizing, which would mean pressing a clear lesson rather than leaving things open. Verbosity and sentimentality lack partners." },
  { id:"se042", type:"se", topic:"se", difficulty:3,
    text:"Although his admirers describe him as principled, the senator's refusal to compromise on even trivial matters strikes many observers as merely {1}.",
    options:["pliable","quixotic","obdurate","tractable","intransigent","venal"],
    answer:[2,4],
    explanation:"\"Merely\" recasts \"principled\" negatively; refusing to compromise even on trivial matters is stubbornness: obdurate and intransigent. Pliable and tractable form a pair meaning easily swayed, the opposite. Quixotic (impractically idealistic) is close but has no partner and stresses idealism rather than inflexibility; venal is unsupported." },
  { id:"se043", type:"se", topic:"se", difficulty:3,
    text:"The committee's praise for the proposal was {1}, coming only after a long list of objections had been aired.",
    options:["effusive","grudging","fulsome","premature","anonymous","reluctant"],
    answer:[1,5],
    explanation:"Praise offered \"only after a long list of objections\" is given unwillingly: grudging and reluctant. Effusive and fulsome form a pair meaning lavish, contradicting the objections. Premature contradicts \"only after\"; anonymous lacks a partner." },
  { id:"se044", type:"se", topic:"se", difficulty:3,
    text:"To call the architect's designs {1} would be unfair; they are spare, but each restrained gesture is carefully considered.",
    options:["austere","barren","florid","ascetic","ornate","impoverished"],
    answer:[1,5],
    explanation:"The sentence concedes the designs are spare but denies they are empty or lacking: barren and impoverished. Austere and ascetic form a tempting pair, but the author accepts that the designs are spare, so calling them austere would not be \"unfair.\" Florid and ornate contradict \"spare.\"" },
  { id:"se045", type:"se", topic:"se", difficulty:3,
    text:"The once-{1} critic has mellowed with age; his recent reviews are notable for their generosity toward young writers.",
    options:["magnanimous","indulgent","acerbic","prolific","obscure","caustic"],
    answer:[2,5],
    explanation:"\"Once\" and \"has mellowed\" imply he used to be harsh: acerbic and caustic. Magnanimous and indulgent form a pair that describes his current generosity, not his former manner. Prolific and obscure lack partners and are unrelated to mellowing." },
  { id:"se046", type:"se", topic:"se", difficulty:3,
    text:"Her account of the negotiations is admirably {1}; she presents each faction's position fairly, without betraying her own sympathies.",
    options:["partisan","exhaustive","dispassionate","tendentious","impartial","laconic"],
    answer:[2,4],
    explanation:"Presenting each side fairly without revealing her sympathies is dispassionate and impartial. Partisan and tendentious form a pair meaning biased, the opposite. Exhaustive could be admirable but has no partner and does not match fairness; laconic is unsupported." },
  { id:"se047", type:"se", topic:"se", difficulty:3,
    text:"The theory's {1} lies in its apparent ability to explain everything; a hypothesis that no observation could contradict, critics note, explains nothing.",
    options:["merit","virtue","flaw","novelty","provenance","weakness"],
    answer:[2,5],
    explanation:"The second clause shows that explaining everything is a defect, because an unfalsifiable hypothesis \"explains nothing\": flaw and weakness. Merit and virtue form a tempting pair, since explaining everything sounds like a strength, but \"apparent\" and the critics' point reverse that. Novelty and provenance lack partners." },
  { id:"se048", type:"se", topic:"se", difficulty:3,
    text:"Rather than {1} the dissenters, the chair invited them to present their objections at length before the full board.",
    options:["placate","appease","indulge","muzzle","applaud","silence"],
    answer:[3,5],
    explanation:"\"Rather than\" contrasts the blank with inviting dissenters to speak at length, so it means suppress: muzzle and silence. Placate and appease form a pair meaning pacify, which is not the opposite of giving them the floor. Indulge and applaud lack partners and are closer to what the chair actually did." }
];
